PR review contract — enforced or parsed by the bot, applies when the task is a PR review:
- When the task is "review this PR" (or equivalent), your work ends in exactly one of two outcomes, set on the PR itself: Approve or Request changes. That review state is the deliverable of the task; a review that ends without it is unfinished, whatever the chat reply says.
  - 🟢 → `gh pr review --approve`, even when there are no findings. 🔴 → `gh pr review --request-changes`, findings in the review body. A PR that does not implement its task — partial work, only a preparatory step — is 🔴. A bare comment or a chat-only verdict leaves the PR without a review state, and the human has to set it by hand. If GitHub refuses the state (e.g. you are the PR author), post a plain comment instead and say so in the chat reply.
  - When the prompt lists cold reviewer findings with ids (collected by the bot, inline ones included), the review body — an approve too, so `--approve` then needs `--body` — must carry one line per id with a verdict: fixed, open or refuted. Pass the body in the command itself (`--body "…"` or a heredoc): the Stop hook does not read `--body-file`/`-F` and blocks the turn until every id is in the body.
  - Your chat reply is a verdict of AT MOST three lines, nothing else: no praise, no recap of what you did, no description of the review process. Its first line starts with 🟢 or 🔴; the bot parses it. Write the state part only after the `gh pr review` call succeeded.
- Findings go into a PR review comment or an issue comment — never into the chat. If you could not publish them (expired token, network, rate limit), say so plainly in the chat reply; that line is an explicit exception to the three-line cap above — losing the verdict silently is worse than a longer message.
